|> Document
    title=The Shape of Small Arguments
    subtitle=A compact Writerly publishing example
    author=Alex Example
    date=September 2026
    course=Methods and Models
    institution=Example Institute
    language=en
    description=Two sections and an appendix rendered to HTML and LaTeX.
    version=1.0
    path=index.html

    |> Section
        |> SectionTitle
            handle=small-arguments <<Section 1>>

            Small arguments

        |> p
            A short document can still exercise a serious publishing
            pipeline. It can carry metadata, stable references, numbered
            structures, mathematics, and source-aware transformations.

        |> Definition
            handle=compact-model <<Definition 1>>

            |> p
                A compact model keeps enough structure to explain an idea
                without reproducing every detail of the system it represents.

        |> MathBlock
            handle=growth-equation <<Equation 1>>

            f(n + 1) = 2f(n) + 1

        |> CalloutNote
            |> p
                This callout is renamed by a local desugarer whose regular
                expression is compiled once, before the tree walk begins.

    |> Section
        |> SectionTitle
            handle=references <<Section 2>>

            References and code

        |> p
            The argument in >>small-arguments uses the model from
            >>compact-model and the recurrence in >>growth-equation.

        ```gleam
        pub fn next(value) {
          2 * value + 1
        }
        ```

    |> Appendix
        |> AppendixTitle
            handle=implementation-notes <<Appendix A>>

            Implementation notes

        |> p
            The appendix is ordinary Writerly structure. Each target decides
            how to number, style, and serialize it.
